    "imageCaption": "AI-created topic illustration. Numerical examples and diagrams are original fictional teaching cases.",
    "html": "<p><strong>Choose an online business idea by testing the customer problem, delivery work, contribution and cash cycle. Services, digital products and ecommerce can each be workable, but an online sale does not remove acquisition, support or collection costs.</strong></p><p>All amounts and operating results below are original fictional teaching examples in USD. They are not customer results, industry averages or forecasts. Use your own dated records and applicable accounting policies.</p><h2 id=\"models\">Choose the operating model before the platform</h2><p>Consider a narrowly scoped editing service, a specialist spreadsheet template or a curated physical accessory. Each needs a defined buyer and evidence that the offer solves a problem. The service sells a delivery commitment; the download sells a usable digital asset; ecommerce adds sourcing, stock and fulfillment. These are examples to test, not a ranking of profitable opportunities.</p><p>A marketplace can supply discovery and payment tools while creating fees, competition and platform dependence. Your own site offers different control but still needs a way to attract qualified customers. Do not choose by the platform headline alone: write who buys, why, what they receive, when they pay and what you must do after the sale.</p><h2 id=\"compare\">Put three models on a consistent cost boundary</h2><div class=\"article-table-scroll\"><table><caption>Original per-sale examples; before fixed overhead, income tax and unpaid owner time</caption><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Model</th><th scope=\"col\">Net price</th><th scope=\"col\">Included variable costs</th><th scope=\"col\">Contribution</th><th scope=\"col\">Resource constraint</th><th scope=\"col\">Cash issue</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope=\"row\">Client service</th><td>$600 engagement</td><td>$90 delivery + $150 acquisition</td><td>$360</td><td>12 owner-hours</td><td>Approval and collection</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Digital download</th><td>$40 sale</td><td>$4 delivery/support + $12 acquisition</td><td>$24</td><td>60 setup hours + support</td><td>Refunds and ongoing updates</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Stocked ecommerce</th><td>$80 order</td><td>$42 goods + $10 delivery/fees + $12 acquisition</td><td>$16</td><td>Inventory and fulfillment</td><td>Supplier payment before settlement</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The service contribution is $600 − $90 − $150 = $360, or $30 per owner-hour over twelve hours. The download leaves $24 per sale. At 100 sales, that is $2,400 before recovering setup work and fixed costs. Valuing 60 creation hours at a selected $25 adds a $1,500 planning requirement; that is an explicit owner-time assumption, not a cash invoice or market wage.</p><p>The ecommerce order contributes $16 after the included costs. A hypothetical $800 monthly fixed commitment requires 50 orders merely to cover that commitment at unchanged unit economics. Refunds, damaged stock and owner pay still need their own included or separately identified treatment. A higher revenue total can coexist with a weaker cash position.</p><h2 id=\"proof\">Collect evidence matched to the model</h2><div class=\"article-table-scroll\"><table><caption>What a small test must reveal</caption><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Model</th><th scope=\"col\">Test</th><th scope=\"col\">Evidence beyond a click</th><th scope=\"col\">Reason to revise</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope=\"row\">Service</th><td>One tightly scoped paid pilot</td><td>Accepted output, time log, collection</td><td>Revisions exceed scope</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Digital product</th><td>Small usable sample and paid release</td><td>Buyer can use the file; support requests</td><td>Purchase depends on misleading claims</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Ecommerce</th><td>Small stock batch</td><td>Net receipts, returns, delivery cost</td><td>Unsold inventory absorbs cash</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Interest is useful but not equivalent to an order. Record prospects who declined as well as buyers. A successful pilot demonstrates that one transaction worked under its conditions; it does not establish a repeatable acquisition cost or a large market. Set a test budget and decision date before seeing the result.</p><h2 id=\"cash\">Trace cash before calling a model low-cost</h2><p>For a separate ecommerce batch of 50 units, paying $42 goods cost per unit uses $2,100 before sales. If only 30 units sell, 20 units remain in stock. Do not expense or ignore that stock solely to make a launch cash estimate match a per-order contribution table. Shipping, acquisition, fees, refunds and settlement delays need their own dates. The <a href=\"/calculators/business-startup-cost-calculator/\">startup cost calculator</a> organizes one-time and recurring cash requirements.</p><p>A digital product avoids physical stock but can still require software, licensed materials, design effort, support and maintenance. A service may require no inventory but be capped by qualified delivery hours. Test the bottleneck that actually constrains the model.</p><h2 id=\"decision\">Choose the next test rather than an earnings promise</h2><p>Reject a model if you cannot deliver it competently, access the intended customers or tolerate its funding gap. Verify relevant professional and local requirements for the actual activity. Do not infer permission to offer regulated services from an idea list. Compare the best-supported alternatives in the <a href=\"/blogs/small-business-ideas/\">general business-idea screen</a>.</p><p>After selecting a model, use <a href=\"/blogs/how-to-start-online-business/\">the online launch workflow</a> to test checkout and delivery. Use the <a href=\"/blogs/business-model-canvas/\">business model canvas</a> to record dependencies. Keep the chosen model provisional until repeat transactions show that its demand, costs and support burden can coexist.</p><h2 id=\"worksheet\">Download the editable worksheet</h2><p><a href=\"/downloads/online-business-ideas-worksheet.html\" download>Download the printable HTML worksheet</a>. It contains the completed case and a separate blank working record. It does not calculate automatically. Edit in your browser, then print or save a completed copy before closing; your edits are not sent to MyBreakeven.</p><h2 id=\"sources\">Sources and scope</h2><p>Primary references below support the stated definitions or framework. The worked case, numbers and decision records are original MyBreakeven material. Reviewed November 6, 2026; no external professional sign-off is claimed.</p><ul><li><a href=\"https://www.sba.gov/counseling/plan-your-business/\">SBA: market research and business planning</a></li><li><a href=\"https://sell.amazon.com/learn/online-business-ideas\">Amazon: examples of online business models; platform-specific context</a></li></ul>",
