    "html": "<p><strong>Start an online business by defining one offer and customer, validating demand, checking the requirements for that activity, and testing a complete payment-to-delivery workflow before scaling. Measure net receipts and delivery capacity alongside sales.</strong></p><p>All amounts and operating results below are original fictional teaching examples in USD. They are not customer results, industry averages or forecasts. Use your own dated records and applicable accounting policies.</p><h2 id=\"offer\">Write a testable offer and acceptance boundary</h2><p>Specify the deliverable, exclusions, net price, turnaround, revision limits and how acceptance is recorded. For the fictional pilot here, a $300 service package takes six delivery hours and $40 job-specific cash costs. Record when the buyer can cancel and how a refund or complaint will be handled. Do not promise outcomes that you cannot substantiate.</p><p>Use <a href=\"/blogs/online-business-ideas/\">the online model comparison</a> if the model is undecided. This guide starts after that choice. It does not require a custom store: the first workflow can be simple if it supports accurate terms, appropriate records and a reliable way to fulfill the offer.</p><h2 id=\"validate\">Run one bounded demand test</h2><div class=\"article-table-scroll\"><table><caption>Original service pilot; all five orders are completed and accepted</caption><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Measure</th><th scope=\"col\">Case value</th><th scope=\"col\">Evidence / boundary</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope=\"row\">Qualified inquiries</th><td>20</td><td>Observed pilot list</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Paid orders</th><td>5</td><td>25% of inquiries in this case only</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Net sales</th><td>5 × $300 = $1,500</td><td>Accepted service package</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Delivery cash costs</th><td>5 × $40 = $200</td><td>Tools and job inputs</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Acquisition test spend</th><td>$250</td><td>One test channel</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Contribution after included costs</th><td>$1,050</td><td>Before owner time and overhead</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Owner delivery time</th><td>5 × 6 = 30 hours</td><td>$35 contribution per delivery hour</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Five divided by twenty is a 25% conversion for this tiny observed pilot, not a forecast or a platform benchmark. The $1,050 is not take-home pay. It excludes owner selling time, general administration, fixed subscriptions, tax and setup work. If delivery takes eight hours instead of six, 40 hours produce $26.25 contribution per delivery hour with other inputs unchanged.</p><p>At a selected 40-hour delivery ceiling, six-hour jobs permit at most six whole packages, before checking demand. Seven need 42 hours. Record a stop rule for spend, late work, refunds and capacity. A profitable five-order sample does not justify multiplying the result by all website visitors.</p><h2 id=\"requirements\">Check the activity and selling locations</h2><p>SBA launch guidance separates registration, IDs, permits, banking and insurance. The IRS startup checklist is explicitly not exhaustive and points to state requirements. Record which official state and local offices apply to your actual business, where customers are served and what evidence remains outstanding. This article does not decide entity choice, tax nexus or professional licensing for you.</p><p>For a business operating at home, also check <a href=\"/blogs/start-home-based-business/\">workspace, property and capacity constraints</a>. For US merchandise orders, the FTC explains requirements concerning advertised shipping times, delay consent and refunds. Read the applicable rule before making a shipping promise. Do not treat that merchandise-specific reference as a universal rule for every digital or professional service.</p><h2 id=\"workflow\">Test order, payment, delivery and reversal together</h2><div class=\"article-table-scroll\"><table><caption>Launch acceptance record</caption><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Stage</th><th scope=\"col\">Test evidence</th><th scope=\"col\">Failure to resolve before wider launch</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope=\"row\">Offer</th><td>Customer sees scope, total and timing</td><td>Hidden costs or unavailable promise</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Payment</th><td>Authorized test matches order amount</td><td>Duplicate charge or wrong currency</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Delivery</th><td>Buyer receives correct item or access</td><td>Broken file or missed handoff</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Reversal</th><td>Refund/credit traced to order</td><td>Refund missing from records</td></tr><tr><th scope=\"row\">Records</th><td>Order, fees and settlement reconcile</td><td>Bank receipt treated as gross sales</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Use authorized test transactions or a processor’s documented test environment; do not fabricate a customer review. Check the workflow on a phone and keyboard. For downloads, open the delivered file on the intended application. For services, test booking and approval. For merchandise, verify packaging, tracking and return handling.</p><h2 id=\"reconcile\">Reconcile the first orders before increasing spend</h2><p>If the processor settles less than gross customer charges, identify fees, refunds, taxes and timing rather than editing invoice amounts to match the bank. Keep gross activity and settlement deductions traceable. Use <a href=\"/blogs/accounts-receivable-process/\">the receivable process</a> for unpaid customer invoices and <a href=\"/blogs/accounts-payable-process/\">the payable process</a> for supplier obligations.</p><p>Prepare a dated cash plan including setup payments, settlement delays and owner withdrawals separately from operating contribution. For the detailed physical-store funding boundary use <a href=\"/blogs/how-much-does-it-cost-to-start-an-ecommerce-store/\">the ecommerce startup-cost guide</a>. Continue with <a href=\"/blogs/business-goals/\">measurable business goals</a> only after the delivery system and records are working.</p><h2 id=\"worksheet\">Download the editable worksheet</h2><p><a href=\"/downloads/how-to-start-online-business-worksheet.html\" download>Download the printable HTML worksheet</a>. It contains the completed case and a separate blank working record. It does not calculate automatically. Edit in your browser, then print or save a completed copy before closing; your edits are not sent to MyBreakeven.</p><h2 id=\"sources\">Sources and scope</h2><p>Primary references below support the stated definitions or framework. The worked case, numbers and decision records are original MyBreakeven material. Reviewed November 6, 2026; no external professional sign-off is claimed.</p><ul><li><a href=\"https://www.sba.gov/counseling/launch-your-business/\">SBA: launch requirements</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.irs.gov/businesses/small-businesses-self-employed/checklist-for-starting-a-business\">IRS: startup checklist and state-level requirements</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.ftc.gov/legal-library/browse/rules/mail-internet-or-telephone-order-merchandise-rule\">FTC: merchandise shipping and delay requirements</a></li></ul>",
